arXiv:2510.04794v1 [cs.CV] 06 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alon Kaya
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>alonkay@post.bgu.ac.il</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Beer Sheva, Israel
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Igal Bilik
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Beer Sheva, Israel
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Inna Stainvas
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>GE Healthcare Science and Technology, Israel
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">Vision-transformers (ViTs)</span> and large-scale <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">convolution-neural-networks (CNNs)</span> have reshaped computer vision through pretrained feature representations that enable strong transfer learning for diverse tasks. However, their efficiency as backbone architectures for geometric estimation tasks involving image deformations in low-data regimes remains an open question.
This work considers two such tasks: 1) estimating 2D rigid transformations between pairs of images and 2) predicting the fundamental matrix for stereo image pairs, an important problem in various applications, such as autonomous mobility, robotics, and 3D scene reconstruction.
Addressing this intriguing question, this work systematically compares large-scale <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> (ResNet, EfficientNet, CLIP-ResNet) with <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span>-based foundation models (CLIP-ViT variants and DINO) in various data size settings, including few-shot scenarios.
These pretrained models are optimized for classification or contrastive learning, encouraging them to focus mostly on high-level semantics. The considered tasks require balancing local and global features differently, challenging the straightforward adoption of these models as the backbone.
Empirical comparative analysis shows that, similar to training from scratch, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> outperform <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> during refinement in large downstream-data scenarios. However, in small data scenarios, the inductive bias and smaller capacity of <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> improve their performance, allowing them to match that of a <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span>. Moreover, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> exhibit stronger generalization in cross-domain evaluation where the data distribution changes.
These results emphasize the importance of carefully selecting model architectures for refinement, motivating future research towards hybrid architectures that balance local and global representations.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, large-scale models, such as <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">convolution-neural-networks (CNNs)</span> and <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">vision-transformers (ViTs)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, have revolutionized the computer vision domain. When trained using large datasets, <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> and <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> enable efficient feature transfer with minimal fine-tuning. They were shown to provide exceptional generalization capabilities and can be efficiently used as frozen or refined backbones for diverse downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, these models were extensively compared in the literature, mostly when trained from scratch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> or fine-tuned for classification problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. It was demonstrated that given sufficient pre-training data, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> outperform <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> when fine-tuned for classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
In addition, differences in feature extraction mechanisms between these models have been investigated, and it was shown that larger <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> enable more robust intermediate representations, thus providing robustness in classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
It was also shown that for segmentation tasks, <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models outperform <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> when trained from scratch on smaller datasets, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. The efficiency of transfer learning from general image classification to more specialized recognition scenarios was demonstrated in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. It was shown that the off-the-shelf features extracted from both pretrained <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> and <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> are suitable for periocular recognition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The limited availability of annotated training data is one of the major practical challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and the <span title="few-shot learning" class="ltx_glossaryref">few-shot learning (FSL)</span> framework was introduced to address it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. <span title="few-shot learning" class="ltx_glossaryref">FSL</span> has been used in various tasks, such as natural language understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, image classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, semantic segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, object detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and others <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This work focuses on <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> and <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> comparison by applying transfer learning to downstream regression tasks involving geometric estimations of image distortions while investigating the influence of training data size, including a minimal few-shot learning framework.
Unlike conventional high-level semantic tasks, such as image classification, segmentation, and object detection, geometric transformation tasks require both more intensive local feature extraction (e.g., for implicit pixel correspondences) and global scene understanding (e.g., scene geometry) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. These requirements challenge the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> adoptions as backbone architectures for the considered geometric estimation tasks.
Two downstream tasks are considered in this work: (i) estimating 2D rigid transformations between pairs of images and (ii) predicting the fundamental matrix, <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix, between a pair of stereo images in an end-to-end manner.
We utilize these tasks across different models to conduct a comparative analysis between <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>, addressing the following related open questions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does the parameter-count difference between <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> affect refinement performance in few-shot settings?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How do differences in pretraining objectives influence performance in these downstream tasks?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> are extensively tested and compared with the state-of-the-art models. It is demonstrated that:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transfer learning using <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> enables efficient feature extraction for accurately estimating geometric transformations, even in a challenging few-shot scenario.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> outperform <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> during refinement in larger-data scenarios, while <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> achieve comparable performance in low-data regimes even when the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> were pretrained on large-scaled datasets.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Smaller patch-size <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> outperform their larger patch-size variants in geometric transformation tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Freezing bottom layers in the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> can enhance its performance during refinement by reducing overfitting in small-data scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All considered models generalize well to an unseen dataset for the fundamental matrix estimation task, and the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> outperform <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> in terms of cross-domain dataset generalization.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The main novelty of this work can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A comparative investigation of transfer learning <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> for geometric transformation estimations as regression tasks with varying dataset sizes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The finding that, even with a deep pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span>, the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> achieves comparable or even superior performance in the low-data few-shot regime during transfer learning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is demonstrated that the fundamental matrix can be accurately estimated between stereo image pairs in a few-shot setting, outperforming other state-of-the-art approaches with comparable training data size.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A proposed network that enables a fair comparison between diverse models for various image deformation estimation tasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The results of this work are expected to contribute to a broad spectrum of applications, such as aerial photography, medical imaging, and autonomous vehicles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>ViT vs. CNN-based Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transformers, originally introduced for natural language processing (NLP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, were adapted to computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> by converting images into a sequence of fixed-size patches that are linearly projected into embeddings, and include a global classification (CLS) token.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Comparison between <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models has been extensively investigated in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> were shown to outperform conventional <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> architectures when trained from scratch or when pretrained using limited data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. This superiority can be attributed to <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>’ inductive bias towards locality by focusing on local connections in the lower layers and weight sharing. <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> commonly have larger capacities and, therefore, require significantly larger data to estimate self-attention parameters accurately <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. When sufficient data is available, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> efficiently capture global dependencies and commonly outperform <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It was also shown that largely-pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> are robust to occlusions and domain shifts and provide higher generalization capabilities using off-the-shelf features for classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
Furthermore, it was demonstrated that smaller patch sizes in <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> enhance their robustness to adversarial spatial transformations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. It was also shown that, unlike <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>, which discard low-level details to achieve discriminative representation in higher layers, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> retain detailed image information across all layers, providing strong feature propagation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
Further, it was shown that spatial information, which is processed in the early layers, is crucial to achieving high performance and that during transfer learning, the lower layers of a <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> remain well preserved in classification tasks 
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The effect of pretraining dataset size on transfer learning performance was investigated, revealing that <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> provide stronger intermediate representations through larger pretraining datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
However, comparative studies between the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models with transfer learning for geometric transformation estimations while analyzing downstream data size have not been conducted in the literature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>The Fundamental Matrix Estimation Problem</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Estimation of the fundamental matrix, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, is one of the key problems in computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. The <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix describes the intrinsic projective relationships between two pin-hole cameras observing the same 3D scene from different viewpoints. For any matching point pair with homogeneous coordinates, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=[x,y,1]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=[x,y,1]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the first, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}=[x^{\prime},y^{\prime},1]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}=[x^{\prime},y^{\prime},1]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the second image, the following epipolar constraint has to be satisfied:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}^{T}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{p}=0\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐅𝐩</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}^{T}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{p}=0\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">meaning the match in the second image lies on an epipolar line determined by: <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{e}=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐅𝐩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{e}=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The fundamental matrix, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined by the camera intrinsic, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the relative pose between cameras, encapsulated by rotation, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and translation, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}=\mathbf{K}_{2}^{-T}[\mathbf{t}]_{\times}R\mathbf{K}_{1}^{-1}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}=\mathbf{K}_{2}^{-T}[\mathbf{t}]_{\times}R\mathbf{K}_{1}^{-1}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> rank-<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matrix that contains the essential geometry between two views. The fundamental matrix facilitates outlier detection in point correspondences by constraining their search to epipolar lines, enhancing stereo matching and 3D reconstruction algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Estimating the F-matrix with deep learning</span>.
<br class="ltx_break">Conventional <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation approaches, such as SIFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and RANSAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, focus on sparse feature matching and robust estimation. Recently, deep learning-based feature matching approaches like D2Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, SuperPoint <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and R2D2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> have also been proposed. Alternative approaches, such as RoMa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and DKM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, exploit dense feature matching by pixel-wise correspondences, improving accuracy in texture-less regions.
The <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation problem is closely related to estimating the pose matrix, which has also been explored using deep learning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Alternative approaches attempt to estimate the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix directly in an end-to-end manner. The deep learning-based approach for the direct <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation was first introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
Siamese-built <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based network and a rank constraint layer for the <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation without point correspondences was proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
The approach for the fundamental matrix estimation from bi-planar radiographs using a specialized end-to-end network, enhancing estimation accuracy, was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
An alternative end-to-end network that integrates feature correspondence with outlier rejection and employs a novel loss function using an evaluation metric more suitable for visual tasks was investigated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
A twin-feature pyramid architecture with an improved attention module was proposed to estimate the fundamental matrix directly from images without correspondences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
This work follows the direct <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation approach, but unlike previous work, it focuses on F-estimation in a data-limited regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work investigates the performance of pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> models for geometric estimation tasks, considering data-limited scenarios, including a few-shot learning framework with only 32 samples. The performance is evaluated for two geometric estimation tasks: 2D rigid transformation and <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation. The first task requires predicting three parameters of 2D geometric transformations: the rotation angle around the image center and the horizontal and vertical translations.
The <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation task involves complex 3D geometric transformations between pairs of stereo images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduces a dedicated network architecture, shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, that enables the fair comparison between the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models. The proposed network consists of two modules: 1) an encoder for feature extraction and 2) a regression for task-specific predictions. The encoder module is kept the same for all considered tasks, and the regression head is designed specifically for each task as detailed in Subsections <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Regression Module ‣ 4 Rigid Transformation Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Regression Module ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. The pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> or <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models were used as the encoder backbone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We consider structurally varied CNN and ViT models, differing in parameter count, pre-training objectives, and training datasets, summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> represent state-of-the-art large-scale foundation models trained using large datasets for various tasks. The considered CLIP-VIT models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> represent vision language models (VLMs) that are trained for text-image alignment with different patch sizes. The <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> with a smaller patch size captures finer image details, providing better image-text alignment performance. The DINO-ViT-B/16 vision model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> relies on contrastive self-distilled learning principles. It is trained using the ImageNet dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, which is significantly smaller than the size of the data used for CLIP training but shares a similar <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> architecture.
These ViT-based models are compared to CNN-based baselines, including ResNet-152 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and
EfficientNetV2-M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, both trained for image classification on ImageNet, and have fewer parameters than the ViT models. We also evaluate CLIP-ResNet-101 and CLIP-ResNet-50x4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, which share the same training objectives as the CLIP-ViTs but use convolutional architectures. Notably, CLIP-ResNet-50x4 has a number of parameters comparable to the ViT-based models, making it particularly relevant for model-size comparisons.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">The considered backbone models. ImageNet1k consists of <math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2</annotation></semantics></math>M images, and the WebImageText dataset contains <math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400</annotation></semantics></math>M image-text pairs collected from the internet. ViT-B/32 and ViT-B/16 denote <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> models with patch size, <math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and SSL denotes the self-supervised learning.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.12" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.12.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Model</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;"># Params</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Training Task</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.12.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S3.T1.12.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Convolutional Networks (CNNs)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.12.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">ResNet-152 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite></span></td>
