<td id="S3.T1.12.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP-ResNet-101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">56.4M</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">WebImageText</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Image–Text Alignment</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.12.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP-ResNet-50X4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">87.2M</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">WebImageText</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Image–Text Alignment</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.12.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S3.T1.12.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Vision Transformers (ViTs)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.12.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP-ViT-B/32 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">88.2M</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">WebImageText</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.8.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Image–Text Alignment</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.12.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP-ViT-B/16 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">86.5M</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.9.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">WebImageText</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.9.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Image–Text Alignment</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.12.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.12.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">DINO-ViT-B/16 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">85.8M</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">ImageNet1k</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.12.10.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">SSL</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04794v1/Network.drawio.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/210;" width="476" height="210" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Schematic representation of the proposed network for comparison between <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> used as the backbone models. The network contains encoder and regression modules. The <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> outputs patch embeddings and an unused CLS token for each frame. The <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> outputs aggregated feature maps. The backbone outputs of both frames are reshaped to a data structure of the size, <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D,\sqrt{N},\sqrt{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D,\sqrt{N},\sqrt{N})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension and <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of spatial patches for <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> or number of pixels in feature maps for <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>. These embeddings are concatenated along the depth dimension and passed through two convolutional layers, followed by location-aware Max-pooling.
The regression module for the <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation task consists of a rank-constraint layer, and the rigid estimation consists of the <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> transformation elements, each using different normalization methods.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Encoder: Feature Extraction Module</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The feature extraction module, optimized via the ablation study detailed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Ablation Study ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, extracts meaningful features from two input images independently. Each image is processed separately using a pretrained model as a Siamese-like network with shared weights. The output of <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> models consists of a series of embedding tokens that correspond to features extracted from image patches and a global CLS token, which is ignored. Each token can interact with every other token from the initial layers of the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span>, creating a global receptive field. Differently, the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> produce aggregated features across increasingly larger receptive fields, with each layer capturing larger spatial relationships.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The feature vectors of the two images are then reshaped to a data structure with dimensions: <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D,\sqrt{N},\sqrt{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D,\sqrt{N},\sqrt{N})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of spatial patches for <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> or number of pixels in feature maps for <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>. These vectors are concatenated depth-wise, forming a unified representation. This representation is processed by two <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> convolutional layers with output channel sizes of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Each layer is followed by ReLU activation and batch normalization to ensure stable and efficient training.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Location-Aware Max-pooling</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The considered tasks rely on preserving the spatial relationships of corresponding image points. Conventional down-sampling operations like Max-Pooling tend to lose this critical location information. Therefore, this work uses the location-aware max-pooling layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> after the convolutional layers. This layer selects the most significant features while retaining their spatial locations by concatenating the max-pool output features with their (normalized) indices. This approach enables the network to maintain spatial information essential for accurate estimations of the considered image deformations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Rigid Transformation Estimation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For planar scenes, rigid transforms are used to simulate a fronto-parallel stereo camera setup. In such cases, the geometric parameters which define the cameras’ relative pose correspond directly to those estimated in the fundamental matrix (F) computation. The idea was to first refine model performance on rigid transform estimation, where abundant synthetic data is available, and then use these refined models to initialize F-estimation. This could have reduced the dataset size needed and alleviated small-data limitations. However, this strategy did not produce improvements, so we did not pursue it further. Instead, this work focuses on comparing architectures directly in the small-data regime.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Regression Module</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed network in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> contains separate regression and encoder modules. The encoder outputs feature vectors with their spatial indices. This output is flattened and then processed by a <span title="multi-layer perceptron" class="ltx_glossaryref">multi-layer perceptron (MLP)</span> with two fully connected layers with feature dimensions of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024</annotation></semantics></math>, each using a ReLU activation function. The <span title="multi-layer perceptron" class="ltx_glossaryref">MLP</span> output is a <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector consisting of the estimated rotation angle and horizontal and vertical shifts. The output values are normalized to the range <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Training and Evaluation Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The network hyperparameters were optimized using the Adam optimizer, selecting the best combination of learning rates from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and batch sizes from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Loss Function</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed loss function combines the <span title="mean-squared error" class="ltx_glossaryref">mean-squared error (MSE)</span> and Huber losses, detailed in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Losses and Evaluation Metrics ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}={\cal L}^{\theta}_{MSE}+{\cal L}^{\theta}_{Huber}+\alpha({\cal L}^{s}_{MSE}+{\cal L}^{s}_{Huber})\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}={\cal L}^{\theta}_{MSE}+{\cal L}^{\theta}_{Huber}+\alpha({\cal L}^{s}_{MSE}+{\cal L}^{s}_{Huber})\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}^{\theta}_{MSE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}^{\theta}_{MSE}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}^{\theta}_{Huber}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}^{\theta}_{Huber}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <span title="mean-squared error" class="ltx_glossaryref">MSE</span> and Huber loss for angle estimations, respectively, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}^{s}_{MSE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}^{s}_{MSE}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}^{s}_{Huber}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}^{s}_{Huber}</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding shift estimation losses. The hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> was empirically set to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, balancing the angle and the shift contributions to the total loss for estimation accuracy optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Performance Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed comparative analysis is performed using translation and rotation error metrics. Translation error is computed as the Euclidean, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, distance, measured in pixels, between the predicted shifts along the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> axes and their corresponding ground-truth values. Rotation error is evaluated using the <span title="mean-absolute error" class="ltx_glossaryref">mean-absolute error (MAE)</span>, in degrees, between the predicted rotation angle and the ground-truth angle. The Euclidean distance and <span title="mean-absolute error" class="ltx_glossaryref">MAE</span> metrics formulas are given in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Losses and Evaluation Metrics ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Dataset</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The considered dataset is a subset of ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, selected for its smaller scale and suitability to our low-data regime. Since the task involves estimating rigid transformations rather than semantic classification, the full ImageNet dataset was unnecessary. The subset provides sufficient visual diversity while reducing computational and data overhead. The experiments were conducted using training data of varying sizes, with smaller sizes being subsets of the larger ones. Models were trained multiple times on disjoint sub-trajectories of the dataset, each with the same size, reporting the mean and <span title="standard deviation" class="ltx_glossaryref">standard deviation (STD)</span> of the performance across all subsets. Each image was resized to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by a random crop of size <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math>. Other data augmentation techniques included brightness, contrast, and Gaussian blur adjustments. The rotation angles were randomly sampled from the range <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-30^{o},30^{o}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-30^{o},30^{o}]</annotation></semantics></math>, while the translations along both the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-axes were sampled between <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-32,32]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-32,32]</annotation></semantics></math>. These values are then normalized to the range of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Performance Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rigid transformation estimation performance as a function of the amount of downstream training data is shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 4 Rigid Transformation Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Notice that the performance of all evaluated models degrades in scenarios with smaller available training data.
The <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> models outperform the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models when trained using a large amount of data, even under identical training objectives and a comparable number of parameters, as demonstrated by the performance gap between the CLIP-ViT-B/16 and CLIP-Res-50X4 models. However, in the low-data regime, the performance of <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> becomes comparable. The <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> with a smaller patch size of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math> outperforms its larger patch-size variant in the majority of experiments. This confirms the claim of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> that a <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> with a smaller patch size is more robust to spatial image distortions.
Furthermore, although the DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> model is pretrained on a relatively smaller dataset, it observes a substantially larger number of image patches during self-supervised learning. This enables it to achieve performance comparable to, or even exceeding, that of CLIP-VIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> in tasks such as rigid transformation estimation. This advantage stems from DINO’s ability to capture <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mid-level features with strong spatial locality</span>, which are particularly beneficial for tasks requiring precise geometric reasoning. Unlike CLIP, which is optimized for global semantic alignment across modalities and tends to lose spatial detail due to its pooling strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, DINO retains richer local structure through its self-distillation objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. Recent studies have shown that DINO features exhibit higher sensitivity to patch-level variations and outperform CLIP in fine-grained perception tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, making them more suitable for dense prediction and transformation estimation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/Affine_Translation.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F2.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/Affine_Rotation.png" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S4.F2.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Rigid transformation estimation performance. (a) Euclidean translation error in pixels. (b) MAE rotation error in degrees. Solid lines represent ViT-based models and dashed lines represent CNN-based models. For the visualization, <span title="standard deviation" class="ltx_glossaryref">STDs</span> are divided by factor, <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Among the evaluated models, CLIP-ResNet-101 achieves the best performance in the low-data regime. This can be attributed to its large-scale multimodal pretraining on hundreds of millions of image-text pairs, its relatively compact architecture compared to Transformer-based models, and the inductive bias introduced by convolutional layers. These factors contribute to its strong generalization ability when training data is limited. However, it is noteworthy that the DINO model achieves comparable performance, likely due to its ability to retain spatially localized mid-level features through self-supervised learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Fundamental Matrix Estimation</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Regression Module</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The regression module for the fundamental matrix estimation in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> maps the extracted features and their spatial locations into a matrix, with the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix’s properties, such as rank <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> degrees of freedom (DOF).
The encoder module’s output is flattened and processed by a <span title="multi-layer perceptron" class="ltx_glossaryref">MLP</span> with two fully connected layers with feature dimensions of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>, using ReLU as the activation function.
The <span title="multi-layer perceptron" class="ltx_glossaryref">MLP</span> produces an <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector that serves as a compact representation of the matrix parameters. These parameters are then used to construct the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix in the subsequent rank-constrained layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. The first six elements of the vector form the first two columns, and the last two elements determine the third column as a linear combination of the first two.
Notice that the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix is scale-invariant. Therefore, a normalization layer is used to enforce this property and ensure numerical stability explicitly, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{\text{normalized}}=\frac{\mathbf{F}}{|\mathbf{F}\|_{F_{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mtext>normalized</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐅</mi><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{\text{normalized}}=\frac{\mathbf{F}}{|\mathbf{F}\|_{F_{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{F_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{F_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix Frobenius norm<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            The ground truth fundamental matrix is normalized the same.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Training and Evaluation Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The network is trained using a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and a batch size of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>, with the Adam optimizer.
The <span title="symmetric epipolar distance" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_font_italic">symmetric epipolar distance (SED)</span> symmetrically quantifies the geometric distances of corresponding points from their respective epipolar lines in the two images. The <span title="symmetric epipolar distance" class="ltx_glossaryref">SED</span> is incorporated in the training loss to enforce epipolar consistency and is used during the test as an evaluation metric:</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SED}(\mathbf{F},\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}^{\prime})=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{1}{\|l_{i}\|^{2}}+\frac{1}{\|l_{i}^{\prime}\|^{2}}\right)(x_{i}^{T}Fx_{i}^{\prime})^{2}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>SED</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SED}(\mathbf{F},\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x}^{\prime})=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{1}{\|l_{i}\|^{2}}+\frac{1}{\|l_{i}^{\prime}\|^{2}}\right)(x_{i}^{T}Fx_{i}^{\prime})^{2}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are homogeneous corresponding points in the two images, and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of point correspondences. The terms <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|l^{\prime}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|l^{\prime}\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|l\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|l\|</annotation></semantics></math> refer to the norms of the epipolar lines, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\prime}=\mathbf{F}^{T}\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\prime}=\mathbf{F}^{T}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐅𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which normalize the epipolar distance, ensuring invariance to the image scale.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Loss Function</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">High-confidence inlier correspondence points with a <span title="symmetric epipolar distance" class="ltx_glossaryref">SED</span> error below <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> were selected using the ground-truth <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix. These points were adjusted according to the resize and crop parameters and used to compute the <span title="symmetric epipolar distance" class="ltx_glossaryref">SED</span> error component of the estimated <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix, which contributes to the overall loss function:</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}={\cal L}_{MSE}+\alpha{\cal L}_{Huber}+\beta{\cal L}_{SED}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}={\cal L}_{MSE}+\alpha{\cal L}_{Huber}+\beta{\cal L}_{SED}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}_{MSE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}_{MSE}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}_{Huber}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}_{Huber}</annotation></semantics></math> are the MSE and Huber loss, calculated using the estimated and ground-truth <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices and detailed in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Losses and Evaluation Metrics ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. The <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal L}_{SED}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal L}_{SED}</annotation></semantics></math> is the SED term defined in (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.2 Training and Evaluation Setup ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), obtained from the estimated <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix. The coefficients, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, balance the contributions of the loss terms, and their optimal values are empirically set to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2 </span>Performance Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fundamental matrix estimation accuracy is evaluated using two metrics: (i) SED, defined in (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.2 Training and Evaluation Setup ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), and (ii) the algebraic distance (AD), which evaluates the absolute error in satisfying the epipolar constraint (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2 The Fundamental Matrix Estimation Problem ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\text{AD}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_{i}^{\top}Fx_{i}^{\prime}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AD</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\text{AD}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_{i}^{\top}Fx_{i}^{\prime}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The two considered metrics demonstrated similar results in all conducted experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two data sets, KITTI Odometry dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> and FlyingThings3D dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, were considered in this work. The KITTI Odometry dataset is commonly used for real-world automotive applications and serves as a standard for testing vision algorithms in realistic settings. The FlyingThings3D dataset contains objects moving along randomized 3D trajectories without naturalistic constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The performance was evaluated using training data of different sizes, obtained by sub-sampling each sequence with a different fraction, ensuring that data of smaller sizes are nested within larger ones. The dataset size ranges from <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2166" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2166</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2166</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> image pairs; sizes below <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> yield impractical SED values. Each sequence in the KITTI dataset was split into three equal, disjoint parts, reporting their mean and <span title="standard deviation" class="ltx_glossaryref">STD</span>.
In the FlyingThings3D dataset, the training sequences were uniformly selected, while the test set remained fixed for both datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Each image is resized to <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math>
pixels and augmented using random cropping to <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224</annotation></semantics></math>
pixels and adjustments to brightness, contrast, and Gaussian blur.
Various ground-truth <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices were generated and estimated based on the crop parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Performance Evaluation</h3>

<section id="S5.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.1 </span>Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training dataset size influence
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4.1 Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the SED and AD of the <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation using <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> models with various downstream training data sizes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/models_kitti.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S5.F3.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/models_kitti_alg.png" id="S5.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S5.F3.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F3.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SED (a) and AD (b) metrics for <math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation using <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> models with various training data sizes on the KITTI dataset. For visualization, <span title="standard deviation" class="ltx_glossaryref">STDs</span> are divided by <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that in scenarios with large training data, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span>-based models consistently outperform <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>, even under identical training objectives and a similar number of parameters. In scenarios with small data sizes, the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> performance is comparable to the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> with a patch size of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>. <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> with smaller patch sizes outperform the ones with larger patch sizes in the majority of tested scenarios due to their finer resolution needed for geometric and spatial transformation-related tasks. DINO-ViT-B/16 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and CLIP-ViT-B/16<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> achieve comparable results, although in the smallest data regime, the DINO model slightly outperforms CLIP-VIT once again, as a result of DINO’s ability to retain strong local fine-grained features. The CLIP-RES-101 model achieves the best performance across the smallest data size, leveraging extensive pretraining on large-scale image–text alignment datasets and a lower number of parameters, which helps mitigate overfitting. According to the paired t-test <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, the observed differences between CLIP-RES-101 and DINO-ViT/B16 are not statistically significant.
These conclusions are illustrated clearly in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.4.1 Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> as well, which compares the models along with their sizes in the largest and smallest data regimes. These findings support the conclusions made in the rigid transformation estimation experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.04794v1/Large_err.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S5.F4.fig1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F4.fig1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span></p></div></div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.04794v1/Small_err.png" id="S5.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S5.F4.fig2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F4.fig2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span></p></div></div>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SED results for the fundamental matrix estimation task on the KITTI dataset, comparing the largest (a) and smallest (b) training data sizes. Bar plots represent SED performance, and line plots indicate corresponding model parameter counts.
In the small-data regime (b), CLIP-ResNet-101 achieves the lowest SED, benefiting from extensive pretraining on image–text alignment using large-scale datasets, and a lower parameter count, which reduces overfitting. Among transformer-based models, DINO-ViT-B/16 slightly outperforms CLIP-ViT-B/16.
In the large-data regime (a), DINO-ViT-B16 achieves the best performance, outperforming all other models, including CLIP-ResNet variants. This highlights the advantage of its self-supervised learning (SSL) objective, particularly in tasks requiring fine-grained geometric reasoning.
Transformers with a patch size of 16 consistently perform well, likely due to better access to local spatial information.
Overall, DINO-ViT-B16 emerges as the most stable model across varying data scales.
Our experiments also demonstrate that CLIP-ResNet-101 and CLIP-ResNet-50x4 perform best among CNN-based models, attributable to their training on large-scale WebImageText datasets using image–text alignment tasks. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The differences between the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models’ performance during refinement can be explained by the difference in the models’ number of parameters and their architecture. This is consistent with results when these models are trained from scratch.
In a large data regime, the self-attention mechanism of the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> provides an advantage over <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>, even when compared to a similarly trained <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> of a similar size. However, with limited data, the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>’ architecture, with its strong inductive bias towards locality, enables efficient extraction of spatial features in the lower layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In contrast, even with a deep pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span>, fine-tuning with limited data can be challenging, as <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> rely heavily on global representations, which may be less effective at adapting to new tasks with small datasets. As a result, the advantage of <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> diminishes under limited data, ultimately leading to performance comparable to that of <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decreasing ViT Capacity During Refinement
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Beyond the architectural difference between <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> and <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span>, differences in the number of model parameters can also affect performance. The larger <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> models are expected to outperform the smaller <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> in large-data scenarios. However, networks with fewer parameters are less likely to overfit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Therefore, models with fewer parameters could be advantageous in small-data scenarios. Figs. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 4 Rigid Transformation Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4.1 Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.4.1 Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show that the larger CLIP-ResNet model outperforms its smaller variant at larger data sizes but underperforms when training data is limited.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This observation is further explored in the following experiments investigating the influence of the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> capacity on the <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation performance for various training data sizes.
Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4.1 Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the <math id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation errors of the CLIP-ViT-B/32 network, whose shallower layers were incrementally frozen to reduce the number of parameters down to a fully frozen network. Notice that in scenarios with extensive training data, fine-tuning some or all <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> layers yields similar errors, outperforming a fully frozen network.
This can be explained by the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span>’s capability to perform task-specific adaptation when sufficient training data is available, outperforming a fixed-feature extractor.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, it was suggested that <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> capture hierarchical features, with bottom layers incorporating more low-level, task-agnostic features, which are essential for strong performance and remain largely preserved in transfer learning. Conversely, the deeper layers encode more global task-specific information. This observation explains the results in Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4.1 Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, where freezing bottom layers does not degrade performance even in large data regimes.
Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4.1 Performance Evaluation Using KITTI Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that models with partially frozen layers outperform a fully trained network during refinement in data-limited scenarios.
This can be explained by the observation that in data-limited scenarios, the bottom layers learn noise, and as a result, freezing these layers mitigates overfitting and stabilizes training. On the other hand, freezing the deeper layers reduces the model parameters. It limits the model’s ability to capture task-specific features and adapt to downstream tasks, leading to degraded performance of the fully frozen network.
These results also support the relative performance improvement of the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNNs</span> with fewer parameters in data-limited scenarios during refinement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/Frozen_low.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S5.F5.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/Frozen_low_alg.png" id="S5.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S5.F5.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SED (a) and AD (b) for <math id="S5.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation using CLIP-ViT-B/32 freezing bottom layers. For visualization, <span title="standard deviation" class="ltx_glossaryref">STDs</span> are divided by <math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.2 </span>Performance Evaluation Using FlyingThings3D Dataset</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, the <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation performance of the pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based networks was evaluated using the synthetic FlyingThings3D dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. This dataset consists of rendered synthetic sequences with different stereo baselines. Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.4.2 Performance Evaluation Using FlyingThings3D Dataset ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> with the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models’ performance. Notice that the ViT-based models outperform the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> in scenarios with a large training size, and their performance is comparable in small-data scenarios. In addition, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> with smaller patch sizes outperform those with larger ones. These results further validate our findings that were made using the KITTI dataset. Notice that the overall errors are higher when using the FlyingThings3D dataset, likely due to the weak texture of images and motion artifacts. Additionally, differences in lighting conditions and occlusions might increase the errors.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/models_flying.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S5.F6.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F6.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F6.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/models_flying_alg.png" id="S5.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<p id="S5.F6.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F6.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SED (a) and AD (b) performance of pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models in <math id="S5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation using transfer learning with the FlyingThings3D dataset.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.3 </span>Cross-Domain Dataset Experiments</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This experiment evaluated the cross-domain generalization capabilities of the considered models for <math id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation using data from different datasets. The models were trained using the source dataset and evaluated using the target dataset. Both the KITTI and FlyingThings3D datasets are used interchangeably as source and target datasets.
This experiment focuses on the best-performing models trained with the largest data size obtained in <math id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation experiments, and evaluates their cross-domain capabilities in two ways.
First, zero-shot testing was performed by directly evaluating the model using the target dataset without any weight updates. Second, the regression module was fine-tuned for only 40 epochs using the smallest data size from the target dataset while keeping the backbone feature extractor frozen.
Fig. <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.4.3 Cross-Domain Dataset Experiments ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows the errors of the fine-tuned and zero-shot models marked as circles.
The performance of the best-performing models, trained and evaluated using the same datasets, is presented as crosses, and red crosses correspond to the source domain and green to the target domain. Green crosses mark the comparison with the cross-domain models, and red crosses mark the models’ performance reference when tested using their source dataset.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F7.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/cross_domain.png" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/293;" width="476" height="293" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.F7.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;"><img src="2510.04794v1/cross_domain_alg.png" id="S5.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S5.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cross-domain performance evaluation. (a) Models were trained using the FlyingThings3D dataset and evaluated using the KITTI dataset. (b) Models were trained using the KITTI dataset and evaluated using the FlyingThings3D dataset. The top row is SED results, and the bottom row is AD results. ’Cross-domain fine-tuned’ refers to the cross-domain evaluation of models fine-tuned with the regression head only, and ’Cross-Domain Zero-Shot’ refers to models evaluated without refinement. ’Source-Best’ and ’Target-Best’ refer to the best-performing models trained and evaluated on the source and target datasets, respectively. We compare the results with ’Target-Best,’ while ’Source-Best’ serves as a reference for the evaluated models’ original performance.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Only the small performance gap between the cross-domain evaluations and the best-performing model fully trained directly using the target dataset with the largest data size demonstrates the models’ ability to generalize effectively to new domains. While both zero-shot and fine-tuned models perform well using the target dataset, the fine-tuned models slightly outperform them.
Notice that the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> outperform the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models in terms of the generalization capabilities.
This is the most prominent when comparing the CLIP-ViT-B/32 with the <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models trained on KITTI and evaluated on FlyingThings3D. While both perform similarly on the source data, the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> outperforms them in the target domain. This suggests that although all models can successfully generalize to an unseen domain, <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViTs</span> architectures may be better suited to adapt their learned representations to diverse visual environments. Surprisingly, models trained on the synthetic FlyingThings3D dataset perform well using real complex data in zero-shot and few-shot scenarios. This can be attributed to the greater representation variability of the synthetic dataset, which allows for better generalization to KITTI data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.4 </span>Comparison with Alternative Approaches</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix estimation performance of the considered <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span> and <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> models trained using the KITTI dataset is compared with the alternative approaches from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> as summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.4.4 Comparison with Alternative Approaches ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. All deep-learning-based methods are trained using similar sizes of data, except for RoMa, which was trained on very large datasets and can not be considered as the few-shot, low-data setting, which is the main scope of this work. Furthermore, it is based on dense matching principles, whereas our approach estimates the <math id="S5.SS4.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix directly.
Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.4.4 Comparison with Alternative Approaches ‣ 5.4 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Fundamental Matrix Estimation ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that transfer-learning for the pretrained <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> and <span title="convolution-neural-network" class="ltx_glossaryref">CNN</span>-based models outperform conventional methods or models trained from scratch for the considered geometric deformation estimation task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comparison of various approaches in terms of SED in a large-data scenario. For a fair comparison, the model with our training scheme is marked with *. The best result is marked in bold. Note that RoMa was trained using approximately <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> million samples.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Previous Methods</span></th>
