<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Losses and Evaluation Metrics</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This Appendix describes the loss functions used for both the 2D rigid transformation estimation and fundamental matrix estimation tasks, as well as the evaluation metrics specific to the rigid transformation task.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Loss Functions</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The training loss function in both tasks includes <span title="mean-squared error" class="ltx_glossaryref">MSE</span> and Huber loss, which measure the difference between the predicted and ground-truth values. The MSE loss penalizes large deviations quadratically, while the Huber loss mitigates the influence of outliers by behaving like <span title="mean-squared error" class="ltx_glossaryref">MSE</span> for small errors and like <span title="mean-absolute error" class="ltx_glossaryref">MAE</span> for larger errors.</p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MSE}(\hat{y},y)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i})^{2}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>MSE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MSE}(\hat{y},y)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i})^{2}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Huber loss}(\hat{y},y)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}(\hat{y}-y)^{2}&amp;\text{for }|\hat{y}-y|\leq\delta\\
\delta(|\hat{y}-y|-\frac{1}{2}\delta)&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Huber loss</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Huber loss}(\hat{y},y)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}(\hat{y}-y)^{2}&amp;\text{for }|\hat{y}-y|\leq\delta\\
\delta(|\hat{y}-y|-\frac{1}{2}\delta)&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> denote the estimated and ground-truth values, respectively, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of samples, and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a threshold parameter controlling the transition between quadratic and linear behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Evaluation Metrics For Rigid Transformation Estimation</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The performance of the rigid transformation estimation task is evaluated using two metrics: <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L2</annotation></semantics></math> distance for translation error and Angle MAE for rotation error:</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{$L2$ distance}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sqrt{(\hat{x}_{i}-x_{\text{gt},i})^{2}+(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{\text{gt},i})^{2}}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext> distance</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mtext>gt</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mtext>gt</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{$L2$ distance}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sqrt{(\hat{x}_{i}-x_{\text{gt},i})^{2}+(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{\text{gt},i})^{2}}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Angle MAE}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\hat{\theta}_{i}-\theta_{\text{gt},i}|\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Angle MAE</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mtext>gt</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Angle MAE}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\hat{\theta}_{i}-\theta_{\text{gt},i}|\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the predicted <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> shifts for the <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th sample, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{gt},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mtext>gt</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{gt},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{gt},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mtext>gt</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{gt},i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding ground-truth values, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted rotation angle, and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{gt},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mtext>gt</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{gt},i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding ground-truth angle.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Ablation Experiment For Feature Extraction Module</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The optimal use of the <span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref">ViT</span> based feature extractor module is obtained by evaluating a series of experiments for the 2D rigid transformation estimation task using CLIP-ViT-B/32 backbone. Various ways to utilize both the patch embedding tokens and the global CLS token were evaluated. Four approaches were evaluated: a) using only the CLS token, b) using all patch embeddings except the CLS token, c) averaging all patch embeddings, and d) applying a convolutional layer with location-aware max-pooling to all patch embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each of these feature extraction methods was applied separately to the input images. We further explored the most effective input combinations, which include the original and transformed images. For each of the four feature extraction methods (columns of Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix B Ablation Experiment For Feature Extraction Module ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), various input combinations were considered: i) using both original and transformed images as inputs, ii) only the transformed image, providing both images with their Hadamard (element-wise) product, and iii) using only the Hadamard product (rows of Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix B Ablation Experiment For Feature Extraction Module ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). This resulted in 16 experimental configurations summarized in Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix B Ablation Experiment For Feature Extraction Module ‣ A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span title="vision-transformer" class="ltx_glossaryref" style="font-size:113%;">ViT</span><span id="A2.T3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;"> feature extraction and input combinations in rigid estimation task. Each cell represents the Euclidean translation metric (T), in pixels, and the MAE angle metric (R), in degrees, in the format T/R. Columns represent various feature token manipulation strategies, and rows represent various input combination approaches.</span></figcaption>
